<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna protoorder, single-function drive: the callees a function tail-calls.

    gcc 15.3 -O2 -fno-pic -no-pie -nostartfiles -static (x86-64) of

        int sink;
        int target(int a, int *b) { if (b) *b = a; sink = a; return a * 3 + 1; }
        int thunk(int a) { return target(a, 0); }            xor esi,esi; jmp target
        int helper(int a, int b, int c) { sink = b; return a + b * c; }
        int pass(int a, int b, int c) { return helper(a, b + 1, c); }   add esi,1; jmp helper

    A decompile of one function has no callee-first run behind it.  Nothing in
    `thunk` reads RAX after the tail call, so the call kept no result and the
    thunk printed as void; `pass` dropped `c`, which it forwards untouched in
    EDX.  The single-function drive now decompiles each callee the function
    tail-calls and states its recovered prototype first, as a callee-first
    decompile-all does.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "prototype-source", option `protoorder`.  Pass 1
    (`protoorder off`) pins the output without any callee statement, pass 2
    (the default) pins the forwarded result and argument.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000">
